WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.590 --> 00:00:03.879
Tom Holland is one of the biggest young stars
in Hollywood today.

00:00:03.879 --> 00:00:08.590
The 25-year-old actor was catapulted into
international stardom when he first suited

00:00:08.590 --> 00:00:12.769
up as Spider-Man in 2016's Captain America:
Civil War.

00:00:12.769 --> 00:00:16.740
Joining the Marvel Cinematic Universe is a
sure-fire way to join the Hollywood A-list,

00:00:16.740 --> 00:00:19.180
and Holland was no exception.

00:00:19.180 --> 00:00:21.030
"Alright, kid.

00:00:21.030 --> 00:00:27.100
You're an Avenger now."

00:00:27.100 --> 00:00:30.880
Since becoming an Avenger, the British actor
has had the chance to show off his acting

00:00:30.880 --> 00:00:36.230
chops in various non-superhero films, including
the gritty war drama Cherry.

00:00:36.230 --> 00:00:41.219
This 2021 flick proved to be Holland's biggest
departure from the wholesomeness of the MCU.

00:00:41.219 --> 00:00:46.730
It follows a young former soldier who struggles
with PTSD after returning home from fighting

00:00:46.730 --> 00:00:47.730
in Iraq.

00:00:47.730 --> 00:00:51.660
Playing such a troubled and nuanced character
was quite the challenge for Holland, too,

00:00:51.660 --> 00:00:55.480
who has since revealed just how much of a
toll the film took on his health.

00:00:55.480 --> 00:01:00.070
In an interview with British GQ, Tom Holland
revealed the health difficulties he faced

00:01:00.070 --> 00:01:01.199
filming Cherry.

00:01:01.199 --> 00:01:05.319
Production began with the scenes depicting
Holland's character in the throes of addiction,

00:01:05.319 --> 00:01:07.890
and his appearance had to reflect that.

00:01:07.890 --> 00:01:13.039
Revealing that his trainer told him to "eat
only 500 calories a day and run ten miles,"

00:01:13.039 --> 00:01:14.039
Holland remembered:

00:01:14.039 --> 00:01:17.399
"I lost around 27lbs by running in a bin bag
every day."

00:01:17.399 --> 00:01:21.920
While this would be a difficult turn for any
actor, it was made worse by the need to bulk

00:01:21.920 --> 00:01:24.650
back up again for his scenes as an army medic.

00:01:24.650 --> 00:01:25.650
Holland shared:

00:01:25.650 --> 00:01:26.650
"It was brutal…

00:01:26.650 --> 00:01:28.509
I got very sick, actually.

00:01:28.509 --> 00:01:31.270
And it's changed my relationship with food
completely."

00:01:31.270 --> 00:01:35.530
The experience was so grueling that Holland
has sworn off doing anything similar in the

00:01:35.530 --> 00:01:36.530
future.

00:01:36.530 --> 00:01:38.189
In the same interview with GQ, he said:

00:01:38.189 --> 00:01:42.079
"I think I would find it very difficult to
find a role that would warrant that sort of

00:01:42.079 --> 00:01:43.630
abuse on my body again."

00:01:43.630 --> 00:01:44.630
Holland elaborated:

00:01:44.630 --> 00:01:50.159
"This role took me to some of the darkest
places I have ever been, emotionally, physically,

00:01:50.159 --> 00:01:52.920
anythingly [...] I would never go back there
again, not for anyone.

00:01:52.920 --> 00:01:56.739
I am pleased I did it, but that door is now
closed and locked."

00:01:56.739 --> 00:02:01.669
Engaging in such an intense physical transformation
for a role is difficult for any actor.

00:02:01.669 --> 00:02:06.549
While Holland may not be taking on such projects
again, many other actors have undergone brutal

00:02:06.549 --> 00:02:09.159
transformations for the sake of their art.

00:02:09.159 --> 00:02:13.900
Christian Bale, for example, might be the
star best-known for his jaw-dropping physical

00:02:13.900 --> 00:02:14.900
transformations.

00:02:14.900 --> 00:02:20.070
He shocked audiences in the 2004 thriller
The Machinist, playing an emaciated insomniac.

00:02:20.070 --> 00:02:25.020
The actor lost 66 pounds for the role and
was fully committed to truly capturing his

00:02:25.020 --> 00:02:26.020
character.

00:02:26.020 --> 00:02:29.769
Shockingly, just six months after filming
wrapped on The Machinist, Bale had to bulk

00:02:29.769 --> 00:02:34.110
up to play Bruce Wayne in the first of Christopher
Nolan's Batman movies, Batman Begins.

00:02:34.110 --> 00:02:39.000
The actor quickly gained weight after his
Machinist role — so much so that Nolan asked

00:02:39.000 --> 00:02:41.159
Bale to actually lose weight yet again.

00:02:41.159 --> 00:02:42.670
Bale later told Train magazine:

00:02:42.670 --> 00:02:45.489
"I gained too much weight in the run-up to
Batman Begins.

00:02:45.489 --> 00:02:50.930
I wasn't the size that Christopher Nolan wanted,
so I had to cut down 20lb or so just before

00:02:50.930 --> 00:02:53.370
shooting — I was a lot beefier at first."

00:02:53.370 --> 00:02:57.000
And this was far from the last time Bale rapidly
changed his physique.

00:02:57.000 --> 00:03:02.019
He went through a similarly rapid transformation
for 2010's The Fighter, playing a man addicted

00:03:02.019 --> 00:03:03.230
to crack cocaine.

00:03:03.230 --> 00:03:07.450
Though a pattern was emerging, Bale has shared
that he didn't actively seek out such physically

00:03:07.450 --> 00:03:08.450
grueling roles.

00:03:08.450 --> 00:03:09.730
He told MTV News:

00:03:09.730 --> 00:03:13.859
"... I didn't take this job because I went,
'Oh, there's a physical transformation needed.'

00:03:13.859 --> 00:03:15.409
I always go, 'Damn!

00:03:15.409 --> 00:03:17.329
There's a physical transformation needed!'"

00:03:17.329 --> 00:03:21.140
Tom Holland and Christian Bale are far from
the only actors who have undergone extreme

00:03:21.140 --> 00:03:25.889
physical transformations for a role, of course,
with the likes of Matthew McConaughey, Charlize

00:03:25.889 --> 00:03:30.410
Theron, and Joaquin Phoenix also known for
dramatically changing their appearances to

00:03:30.410 --> 00:03:31.410
suit a character.

00:03:31.410 --> 00:03:34.950
Of course, you've got to admire these stars
for their commitment to their craft, though

00:03:34.950 --> 00:03:39.010
it's hard to envy them for the toll it takes
on their bodies — and nobody could blame

00:03:39.010 --> 00:03:41.790
Holland for passing on the chance to do it
again.

